WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.740
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers. Episode 119. It's July 2nd, 2026.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:13.780
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers of space and science. A shoebox size probe will

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:20.200
soon ride an asteroid fly by to test surface operations before the rock skims Earth in 2029.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.120
The European Space Agency has authorized Airbus, Defence and Space to begin full development of

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.700
the AOLIS-2 wind monitoring satellite as a follow-on to the original AOLIS mission.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.820
AOLIS-2 will be developed in partnership with UMETSAT to deliver operational global wind data

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:46.180
for improved weather forecasts and longer-term climate records. The first AOLIS satellite

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:52.600
already demonstrated the first space-based wind lidar measurements from orbit. Engineers will now

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.360
refine the instrument design and spacecraft bus for the next generation platform.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:03.460
The next milestone is the system requirements review that will lock in final performance targets.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:11.300
On a different note, the European Space Agency has selected EMXYS to build Dawn Quixote.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.500
The first CubeSat designed to land and operate directly on an asteroid.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.740
The shoebox size spacecraft will be released by the Ramsey's mission onto a

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:29.540
office ahead of the asteroid's 13 April 2029 Earth flyby. Once on the surface, Dawn Quixote will

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.160
test mobility and measurement techniques in the asteroid's microgravity environment.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:40.260
The contract covers design, build, and integration with the larger Ramsey's spacecraft.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:46.720
Data from the lander will help refine models of asteroid internal structure ahead of any future

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.640
deflection demonstrations. The European Space Agency's placement of a CubeSat on a

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:59.000
office supplies the first opportunity to examine surface mechanics during a close planetary encounter.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:06.360
Dawn Quixote travels aboard the Ramsey spacecraft and separates prior to the 2029 flyby,

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.100
so it can touch down in weak gravity and evaluate mobility hardware.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.160
Measurements of surface composition and internal structure will feed directly into deflection

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:23.660
models for future hazardous asteroids. The effort also shows how compact, inexpensive landers

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:31.780
can supplement data from larger orbiting platforms. The NSF DOE Varacy Ruben Observatory has begun

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:38.360
full science operations for the legacy survey of space and time. More than 80 Japanese researchers

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.380
are already analyzing early data through Subaru Telescope collaboration agreements.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.280
Japanese engineers contributed software pipelines and detector calibration methods developed

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:56.460
during Subaru operations. The wide field LSST camera will repeatedly image the southern sky to

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.820
track transients and map dark matter distributions. Follow-up observations with Subaru's detailed

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:08.580
instruments are expected to sharpen discoveries from the survey's first year. Researchers have

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:14.860
identified a new class of super puff exoplanets with radii similar to Jupiter yet densities lower

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:21.100
than cotton candy. These worlds orbit close to their stars, where intense radiation should strip

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:28.180
atmospheres, yet the envelopes remain inflated. Transit and radial velocity measurements supplied the

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:34.360
size and mass values that revealed the extreme low densities. The discovery challenges standard

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:42.180
models of how gas giants retain heat and mass during migration. Follow-up spectroscopy with large

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:48.240
telescopes will test whether the low densities come from extended hazy layers or internal heat sources.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:55.020
A University of Alabama in Huntsville study proposes that charged dust particles near the sun

00:03:55.020 --> 00:04:01.440
help transport energy into the corona. The astrophysical journal paper models how these grains

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.820
interact with the solar magnetic field and plasma waves. Observation show the corona reaches

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:15.080
millions of degrees while the surface stays at roughly 5,500 degrees Celsius, a long standing puzzle.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:21.080
Dust trajectories calculated in the new work suggest repeated collisions and electromagnetic

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:28.160
interactions could supply the missing heat. Future Parker solar probe passes closer to the sun while

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:34.800
search for direct signatures of these dust plasma couplings. Astronomers have compiled rotation periods

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.440
and activity levels for thousands of stars to improve target selection for habitable zone planets.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:46.700
The catalog draws on data from multiple ground-based surveys and space photometry missions.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:53.180
Stars with lower activity produce steadier signals making small planetary atmospheres easier

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:58.780
to detect. The work directly supports planning for future missions that will measure transmission

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:05.660
spectra of rocky worlds. The next step is cross-matching the catalog with upcoming transit surveys to

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:12.640
prioritize the quietest host stars. NASA's James Webb Space Telescope has examined a planet orbiting

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:19.080
the remnant of a sun-like star that has become a white dwarf. The data revealed the planet's

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:25.160
atmosphere and orbital distance remained intact despite the star's earlier expansion and mass loss.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:31.560
Spectra show molecular features that survive the intense radiation and dynamical upheaval of

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:37.940
the stellar transition. The system offers a rare glimpse of post-main sequence planetary evolution.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:44.200
Additional web cycles will map temperature variations across the planet's dayside to refine

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:50.960
survival models. Measurements from the Chandra X-ray Observatory indicate the outer spiral arms of

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:58.020
the Milky Way reach wider than earlier maps showed. Astronomers used precise distances to dust clouds

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:04.640
absorbing X-rays to trace arm locations. The new positions imply the galaxy's disc is more

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:11.500
extended than standard models assumed. Refined arm geometry will affect calculations of the galaxy's

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:18.400
total mass and rotation curve. Follow-up observations with optical and radio telescopes will test the

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:24.920
revised structure. If the Milky Way were scaled to the size of a dinner plate, a passing galaxy the

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:30.280
size of a saucer would stretch our outer stars into ribbons hundreds of plate widths long.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:36.700
During a close flyby the gravitational gradient across a galaxy pulls stars on the

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:43.700
near side forward while stars on the far side lag behind creating two opposing streams of material.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.980
These tidal tails can extend tens to hundreds of thousands of light years and persist for hundreds

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:56.720
of millions of years after the encounter. The process moves stars at speeds of tens of kilometers per

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:02.180
second relative to the parent galaxy. Enough to fling some into intergalactic space.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:08.540
We still do not know exactly how much dark matter is needed to keep the tails coherent versus letting

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:15.760
them disperse into the cosmic web. A physicist at Texas Tech University used a NASA satellite in an

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:22.220
unconventional observing mode to verify a massive exoplanet. The confirmation relied on timing

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:28.660
variations in the host star's light rather than traditional radial velocity methods. The planet's

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:34.940
mass places it in the super Jupiter category well above the deuterium burning limit. The technique

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:41.680
opens a new pathway for characterizing wide orbit giants around bright stars. Additional observations

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:47.440
are planned to measure the planet's atmospheric properties. The monthly guide highlights a pre-dawn

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:53.980
alignment of the moon with planets. The return of a comet prime Milky Way viewing and a new viewing angle

00:07:53.980 --> 00:08:00.300
on Saturn's rings. Observers in the northern hemisphere can watch the comet climb higher each morning

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:06.740
before dawn. Saturn's rings appear edge on from Earth this month, reducing their apparent brightness.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:14.220
The guide also notes the best times to spot the galactic center low in the south. Monthly updates

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:20.720
continue to track these changing geometries. The popular What Did Hubble See on your birthday page

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:27.780
now features an updated design and expanded search options. Users can select any date and receive five

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:34.160
images captured by the telescope on that calendar day across its mission history. The refresh site

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:40.340
includes improved navigation and larger preview thumbnails. The underlying archive draws from

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:46.160
more than three decades of observations. The update makes the long-running public engagement tool

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:52.580
easier to explore on mobile devices. Remember the show covered Artemis on yesterday's episode.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:59.060
A fabric fragment from the 1903 right flyer flew around the moon aboard Artemis, too,

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:05.540
and has now been returned to the National Air and Space Museum. The momentum joins other mission

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:12.220
keepsakes that link early aviation with current lunar exploration. NASA documented the artifacts

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:16.940
journey to demonstrate continuity between historic and modern flight achievements.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:22.460
The swatch will be displayed alongside other flown objects in the museum's collection.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:30.380
Future Artemis missions plan similar legacy items. ULA's Atlas V lifted off from Cape Canaveral,

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:36.920
carrying 29 Amazon LEO broadband satellites on its final mission with a customer payload.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:43.020
The 551 configuration flight marked the end of that variant's operational use.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:48.140
The satellites join the growing constellation providing global internet coverage.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.240
Ground teams confirm successful deployment and initial orbit insertion.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:58.520
Remaining Atlas V vehicles will now support only national security missions.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:05.800
A Falcon 9 launched from Vandenberg Space Force Base carrying another batch of 24 star-linked spacecraft.

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:10.820
The first stage booster completed its recovery sequence after separation.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:15.740
Deployment was confirmed shortly after the upper stage reached the target orbit.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:22.140
The mission adds capacity to the constellation serving users in the Americas and Atlantic regions.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:28.260
SpaceX continues to increase launch cadence for the network. A major June release

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:35.600
added individual description panels for every one of the 4,716 stars now catalogued in the app.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:42.220
The welcome screen and tutorial were also completely revised to improve first-time user experience.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:48.920
The tool lets the public explore known exoplanet systems with updated orbital and physical data.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:55.100
Developers incorporated new discoveries from recent transit and radial velocity surveys.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:11:00.680
Further updates are scheduled to keep the visualization current with ongoing observations.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:07.060
Next time, keep an eye on new data from the Parker Solar Probe's upcoming close passes as it searches

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:12.320
for dust signatures near the Sun. That covers today's space and science news.

